1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,100
Module 3, Video 3.5, Review projects against the architecture

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:10,870
Right. So the architecture review gate, I mean, this is a short, consistent set of questions every project passes through before funding,

3
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:18,759
and it's what turns principles and reuse from, you know, good intentions into the actual path of least resistance for your ministry.

4
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:22,960
Okay, because we often hear about governance boards holding authority, right?

5
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:27,450
Yeah. But, uh, what does a board actually do with that authority in practice?

6
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:32,460
Like having principles documented somewhere doesn't just automatically change how a government ministry operates.

7
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:37,420
Exactly, yeah. That authority is exercised precisely at this architecture review gate.

8
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:44,920
Right. Every significant new project in your program must pass through these short, consistent questions to align with P-A-E-R-A or PAERA.

9
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:45,839
PAERA, got it.

10
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,720
Yeah. And it has to happen before it receives its funding.

11
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:56,460
That sequence is critical. You know, the gate is where the architecture does its actual work, shifting from, uh, abstract theory to hard decision-making.

12
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:00,960
So how does this gate avoid becoming just another administrative hurdle?

13
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:06,920
I mean, if it operates as a sprawling checklist, projects will just tick the boxes without changing their fundamental design.

14
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:09,640
What specific questions are they actually asking?

15
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:12,280
Well, it is a strict set of five questions.

16
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:15,120
Yeah. And they're asked the exact same way every time.

17
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:16,070
Okay, what's the first one?

18
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:19,000
First, does a shared building block already exist for this?

19
00:01:19,800 --> 00:01:24,980
Second, what data domains do you touch, and do you consume the owner's copy?

20
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:30,450
Wait, let me unpack that second one. By consuming the owner's copy, you prevent ministries from, uh,

21
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:41,400
duplicating data, right? Precisely. Like if a project needs citizen addresses, they must pull from the central register rather than creating a new isolated database that just immediately goes out of

22
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:41,620
date.

23
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:43,760
Correct. You consume the single source of truth.

24
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:46,360
And the third question reinforces this logic.

25
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:51,580
It asks, do you meet the architecture principles like security by design and once only?

26
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:55,160
Right, okay. The fourth asks, is your sourcing choice deliberate?

27
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:58,930
Deliberate sourcing. What does that actually require in this context?

28
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:04,840
Because if a project has a budget, they typically just source whatever commercial software meets their immediate need.

29
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:09,020
Yeah, but deliberate sourcing means the project must justify their choice.

30
00:02:09,060 --> 00:02:17,630
Whether it's a commercial vendor, an open source solution, or an in-house build, it has to be based on the long-term architecture strategy, not just immediate convenience.

31
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:18,400
Oh, I see.

32
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:20,549
Which leads directly to the fifth question:

33
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:23,880
Can you export your data in an open format?

34
00:02:23,940 --> 00:02:27,220
I can see a project manager under a tight deadline pushing back there.

35
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:35,600
Oh, absolutely. Forcing them to build data export capabilities in an open format, well, that requires upfront effort that slows them down.

36
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:41,960
It does, but it is non-negotiable. Asking how data is exported in an open format prevents vendor lock-in.

37
00:02:42,840 --> 00:02:48,220
If you buy a proprietary system and cannot easily extract your data later, the vendor owns your system.

38
00:02:48,340 --> 00:02:56,400
Wow. Yeah. Consistency in asking these five questions makes the gate fair and predictable, so projects prepare for it rather than resent it.

39
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:02,489
That makes sense. But why rely on this specific gate instead of, say, tightening standard procurement rules?

40
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:07,360
You could theoretically force agencies to share systems right at the purchasing level.

41
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:10,230
You could try, but inside a single project,

42
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:16,140
building your own custom solution is almost always faster than trying to integrate with a shared system.

43
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:19,780
Procurement rules cannot override that basic incentive.

44
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:22,070
Right. People take the path of least resistance.

45
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:26,900
Exactly. Only whole of government planning makes reuse rational.

46
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:30,490
The gate is where that whole of government view meets the final decision.

47
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:38,580
Gotcha. When a project proposes to build what already exists, the board uses this gate to issue a clear directive: Consume the shared building block.

48
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:40,720
Okay, but what happens when a project is right?

49
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:45,260
Like a shared platform genuinely cannot do what a specific project needs yet.

50
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:49,400
If the gate is an impenetrable wall, it just stalls critical services.

51
00:03:49,500 --> 00:03:53,600
Well, the gate is not a wall. It is designed to grant exceptions when justified.

52
00:03:53,660 --> 00:04:00,200
However, every single exception must be written down, it must include a specific reason, and it must have a sunset date.

53
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:07,400
An expiry date. I guess that prevents an exception from silently becoming the new permanent normal for that system.

54
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:14,200
Yes, exactly. Ten open-ended exceptions silently become the exact fragmentation you are trying to prevent.

55
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:22,070
Exceptions must be temporary by default, forcing the project to migrate to the shared building block once the platform's capabilities mature.

56
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:25,760
So how do you maintain that consistency over time?

57
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:32,900
Staff turns over, leadership changes. You need a way to remember why an exception was granted, like three years ago.

58
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:34,880
That is managed through the decision log.

59
00:04:35,360 --> 00:04:37,020
This log is the architecture's memory.

60
00:04:37,780 --> 00:04:42,600
Every ruling and every exception goes into this repository with its exact reasoning.

61
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:51,420
So years later, when someone asks why the examination authority cannot have its own learner list, the answer isn't lost just because the architect who made the decision moved on.

62
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:53,520
The answer is preserved in the log.

63
00:04:54,140 --> 00:05:00,620
That written precedent is what keeps the gate consistent year after year, ensuring new projects are treated fairly based on established history.

64
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:03,310
That written precedent really anchors the whole process.

65
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:04,160
Build the gate.

66
00:05:05,040 --> 00:05:07,480
A few questions asked of every project the same way.

67
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:12,580
A clear ruling consume the shared block or a written time boxed exception.

68
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:15,120
Every decision logged with its reason.

69
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:16,600
Sources are in the video description.
